== 2607.15632v1 | Both directions of Fuglede's conjecture fail in dimension two | Tao Zhang | math.CO | 18 pages
   Fuglede's conjecture asserts that a measurable set of positive and finite measure is spectral if and only if it tiles Euclidean space by translations. Counterexamples are known in every dimension $d\ge3$, whereas the one- and two-dimensional cases have remained unresolved. We construct two explicit $60$-point subsets of the rank-two finite Abelian group $\Z_{60}\times\Z_{12}$: one is a translational tile with no spectrum, and the other is spectral but does not tile. A finite-to-infinite transference principle lifts them to bounded subsets of $\R^2$ that are finite unions of unit squares. Consequently, both implications in Fuglede's conjecture fail in dimension two.
== 2609.15720v1 | The stable Bernstein theorem in $\mathbb{R}^{7}$ | Han Hong, Haizhong Li, Gaoming Wang | math.DG | 85 pages. Mathematica verification code: https://github.com/wgaom/stable-bernstein-R7. All comments are welcome
   We give a Green-function proof of the stable Bernstein theorem in $\mathbb R^7$ for smooth, connected, complete, two-sided minimal hypersurface, thus resolving the last case in stable Bernstein problem.
== 2610.37817v1 | The generalized Cartan-Hadamard conjecture | Marcos Agnoletto, Márcio Fabiano Da Silva, Stefano Nardulli, Reinaldo Resende | math.DG | 
   We establish the generalized Cartan-Hadamard conjecture, including rigidity in the equality case. As a corollary, we obtain that isoperimetric regions are bounded. We prove the conjecture for Alexandrov spaces with two sided curvature bounds as well. A sharp Michael-Simon-type inequality for hypersurfaces in these spaces is also shown.
== 2611.04599v1 | Kaplansky's conjectures fail for torsion-free hyperbolic groups in positive characteristic | Nidhal Mghirbi, Seth Douglas | math.GR | 52 pages, 1 figure, 3 tables. First released on Zenodo on 14 October 2026 (v1.0.0, doi:10.5281/zenodo.23374264); this is v1.0.3 (all versions: doi:10.5281/zenodo.23374263). Lean 4 formalization of the
   Do Kaplansky's group-ring conjectures hold for torsion-free word-hyperbolic groups? We show that all four fail in positive characteristic. For every prime $p$ there is a finitely presented, torsion-free, word-hyperbolic group $G$ with a finite two-dimensional classifying space and $a,b\in\mathbb{F}_p[G]$ with $ab=1\neq ba$. So, over every field of characteristic $p$, the group algebra of $G$ is not directly finite and has a non-trivial idempotent, zero divisor and unit, and $G$ is neither sofic nor surjunctive. For $p=2$, $G$, $a$ and $b$ come from OpenAI's random construction, and we add that $G$ is hyperbolic with probability $1-o(1)$, as are OpenAI's zero-divisor groups. For odd $p$ the groups are new: they adapt OpenAI's direct-finiteness construction with a signed cancellation, found first by S. V. Ivanov and independently by us, via an exact solution of a colour-balance problem and a Perron certificate. As far as we know, they are the first torsion-free counterexamples to direct finiteness and to the idempotent conjecture in odd characteristic. Hyperbolicity comes from one deterministic criterion: if a labelled graph is pattern-free, the group obtained by coning off its components is aspherical and has a linear isoperimetric inequality. We prove it by running OpenAI's exclusion of reduced spheres on van Kampen discs, an accounting OpenAI used for another complex. For every prime $p$, the existence of $G$, $a$ and $b$ and the failure of all four conjectures are formalized in Lean 4, including existence forms of the random-graph estimates used. In characteristic zero, where the idempotent and direct-finiteness conjectures hold for torsion-free hyperbolic groups, we show that no cancellation built into the vertex types survives the decay estimate that makes the groups torsion-free. OpenAI's characteristic-two counterexample with torsion is a CAT(0) group.
== 2609.11980v1 | A Metric with Positive Sectional Curvature on $S^3\times S^3$ | Shengtao Guo, Ethan X. Fang, Junwei Lu | math.DG | 
   We construct a smooth Riemannian metric with strictly positive sectional curvature on \(S^3\times S^3\). Since \(S^3\times S^3\) is even-dimensional and has Euler characteristic zero, our result disproves the positive-curvature case of Hopf's sign conjecture. The metric and the proof were discovered by the Odin Automatic AI Research Agent.
